        <div id="by-category" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How Black Friday Discounts Differ by Category</h2>
<p>
            "Up to 50% off fitness equipment" is a banner, not a description. In practice each
            category on this page discounts in its own way, and knowing the pattern tells you which
            badges to believe. These are general patterns we see across the market, not predictions
            for 2026.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Dumbbells, plates and bars are priced close to the metal.</strong> Iron and steel
            are heavy to ship and the margins are thin, so selector dumbbells and plates rarely see
            deep cuts. A real Black Friday offer here usually looks like 10% off, free shipping on
            an order that would normally carry a freight charge, or an add-on (a stand, an expansion
            kit) included. A 40% badge on a plate set is far more likely to be measured from an
            inflated "was" price than to be a genuine 40% reduction. Our <a href="/best-adjustable-dumbbells/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">adjustable dumbbell guide</a> lists the normal selling prices to compare against.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Connected cardio moves the most, and much of it is bundles.</strong> Bikes,
            treadmills and rowers with screens or app subscriptions are where the large headline
            numbers appear, because the maker earns on the membership afterwards. Some of that
            discount is real money off the machine; some of it is a month or a year of membership
            counted at full retail, or accessories you would not have bought. Price the machine
            alone and treat the rest as a bonus. Our sibling pages on <a href="/black-friday-exercise-bike-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Black Friday exercise bike deals</a> and <a href="/black-friday-elliptical-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">elliptical deals</a> go deeper on that category.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Non-connected cardio from specialist makers barely moves.</strong> The Concept2
            RowErg is the clearest example: a mid-October price tracker had it on Amazon, sold by
            Concept2, at the same $990 Concept2 charges itself. Equipment with that kind of
            reputation does not need Black Friday, and waiting for it costs you a month of use.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Racks and benches go on sale in brand-run windows.</strong> Specialist strength
            brands increasingly run their own month-long events rather than a single Black Friday
            price. When we read REP Fitness's Black November page on 28 October, it listed "Rack
            Week" as the first window, starting 2 November 2026, with dumbbells, <a href="/best-adjustable-kettlebells/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>kettlebells</a>,
            benches and cable machines marked "dates coming soon" and no discounts stated. That
            page is about REP's own store; it says nothing about Amazon listings, which may or may
            not follow.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Recovery gadgets and small electronics take the big percentages.</strong> Massage guns, compression boots and <a href="/best-smart-scales/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>smart scales</a> are cheap to ship and frequently
            promoted, so percentage discounts on them are common at sale events, and outgoing
            generations are cleared at the same time. That makes the generation and the reference
            price the things to check, not the size of the badge.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Flooring is a commodity.</strong> Rubber mats and tiles sell on price per square
            foot, and the cheapest source is often not an online sale at all: a farm store's
            3/4-inch stall mat is a well-known alternative. Our <a href="/best-gym-flooring/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">gym flooring guide</a> works out what each option costs per square foot.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="timeline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>When the 2026 Deals Start</h2>
<p>
            The fixed dates are simple. Thanksgiving is Thursday 26 November 2026, Black Friday is
            Friday 27 November and <a href="/cyber-monday-treadmill-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Cyber Monday</a> is Monday 30 November. Everything else is a
            pattern rather than a schedule.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Amazon.</strong> Amazon's Black Friday week typically starts in the week before
            Thanksgiving and runs through Cyber Monday. Amazon had not announced its 2026 dates when
            we checked on 28 October, so treat any article giving a precise start time as a guess
            until Amazon confirms it. Individual early deals and lightning deals often appear before
            the event begins.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Specialist brands.</strong> REP Fitness's Black November page, read on 28
            October, has Rack Week starting 2 November and later windows still to be dated, with a
            note that each window is limited. Other strength and cardio brands run comparable
            early events, often announced only to email subscribers first. If a brand on your list
            does this, signing up is the cheapest way to find out when its window opens.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Cyber Monday and the week after.</strong> Some offers carry over or are
            replaced by different ones on Cyber Monday. The main treadmill hub, <a href="/black-friday-treadmill-deals-2026/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Black Friday treadmill deals 2026</a>,
            tracks that for cardio machines. For home gym equipment, December is usually quieter,
            and January brings its own New Year promotions, which is worth remembering if nothing
            on your list reaches your price in November.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What to do now.</strong> Choose the product, note today's price and seller,
            write down the price at which you will buy, and check stock. The last point matters
            more for strength equipment than for anything else: popular racks, plates and
            dumbbells sell out in sale windows, and a back-ordered item at a discount may arrive
            in January anyway.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="freight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Freight vs Parcel: What the Delivered Price Hides</h2>
<p>
            A home gym deal is only as good as its delivered price, and the seven items here arrive
            in very different ways. Check the delivery terms on the listing before you compare
            prices, because a $50 discount disappears quickly into a freight surcharge or a day of
            carrying boxes.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Parcel items.</strong> A massage gun, a pair of light dumbbells or a <a href="/best-foam-rollers/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>foam roller</a> comes with an ordinary carrier and is left at the door. These are the easiest Black
            Friday purchases: the price you see is close to the price you pay, and a return is a
            matter of printing a label.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Heavy parcels.</strong> Heavier <a href="/best-dumbbell-sets/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>dumbbell sets</a>, rubber sheets and smaller cardio
            machines often still ship by parcel carrier, but heavy ones. Rubber-Cal lists its 4 x 6
            ft Maxx-Tuff sheet at 80 lb; a 50 lb pair of dumbbells weighs 100 lb before the stand.
            They will usually be left at the door or the kerb, and getting them to a basement is your
            job. The Concept2 RowErg weighs 57 lb with standard legs, closer to this group than to a
            treadmill, but check the delivery terms on the listing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Freight.</strong> Treadmills, many connected bikes and many racks and plate
            orders often arrive on a pallet or by a freight carrier who books a delivery window. Horizon
            lists the T101 Connect at an estimated 220 lb shipping weight in a box about 73 inches
            long. Free freight usually means kerbside or the first dry area inside the door;
            room-of-choice delivery and assembly are paid extras on some listings and absent from
            others. Our <a href="/treadmill-delivery-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">delivery guide</a> explains each service level.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two things follow for sale shopping. First, compare delivered prices: a brand store
            that includes freight can beat a lower Amazon price that charges for it, and the
            reverse is just as common. Second, a sale-period return on a freight item is a scheduled
            pickup, often after disassembly, so buy the big pieces only when you are sure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="recalls" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Recalled Equipment to Avoid, New or Used</h2>
<p>
            Sale periods clear old stock, and the second-hand market fills up with equipment people
            are replacing. Both are where recalled products resurface. None of the items below is on
            this page, and we would not buy any of them new or used unless the recall remedy has
            been completed and you can confirm it.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>BowFlex SelectTech 552 and 1090 dumbbells.</strong> Recalled in June 2025
              because the <a href="/best-weight-plates/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>weight plates</a> can dislodge from the handle during use. Johnson Health Tech,
              which now owns BowFlex, issued the recall; the notice names those two dumbbell models
              only. They are still some of the most familiar adjustable dumbbells on the second-hand
              market.</li>
            <li><strong>NordicTrack RW900 rower.</strong> Recalled by iFIT in October 2025, model
              number NTRW19147, because the console can overheat and ignite. The remedy is a
              repair.</li>
            <li><strong>Peloton Bike, model PL01.</strong> Recalled in May 2023 because the seat post
              can break during use. Owners are offered a free replacement seat post.</li>
            <li><strong>Peloton Original Series Bike+, model PL02.</strong> Recalled in November
              2025, for serial numbers beginning with "T", for the same seat-post hazard.</li>
            <li><strong>Sperax walking pads.</strong> The CPSC told owners to stop using them in
              April 2026 after reports of uncontrolled speed, falls and fires; the importer
              refused a recall.</li>
            <li><strong>Horizon T101-05 treadmill.</strong> Recalled in October 2022 for unexpected
              speed changes and stops, fixed with a software update. The T101 Connect on this page is
              a later model and is not covered, but older T101 versions still appear on Amazon, so
              check the model on any discounted T101 listing.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            For anything not on this list, search the brand and model at cpsc.gov before you pay,
            and for a used machine, ask for the model and serial number and check them yourself.
            A deep discount on a model the brand has quietly replaced is a reason to look harder,
            not a reason to hurry.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buy-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What to Buy First</h2>
<p>
            Black Friday rewards a plan and punishes a basket. The order below is how we would
            spend a first home gym budget, on the reasoning that you should buy what you will use
            several times a week before anything that is nice to have.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>1. One loadable strength tool.</strong> For most people that is a pair of
            adjustable dumbbells: they cover pressing, rowing, squatting and carrying in the space of
            a coffee table. Because they rarely discount deeply, there is a good argument for buying
            them before the sales and spending the waiting time training.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>2. The floor under it.</strong> A rubber mat costs little and protects both the
            floor and the dumbbells. Buy it with the first strength purchase, not after the first
            dropped weight.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>3. A bench, then a rack if you want a barbell.</strong> A bench multiplies what
            dumbbells can do. A rack only makes sense once you have decided to train with a barbell,
            and it brings plates, a bar and collars with it, so price the whole set rather than the
            rack. Our <a href="/best-squat-racks-for-home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">squat rack guide</a> covers ceiling height and bolting before you commit.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>4. One cardio machine you will actually use.</strong> This is where the big
            Black Friday numbers are, and also the biggest regrets. Choose the type first —
            treadmill, bike or rower — from what you enjoy and what your room and neighbours can
            take, and only then look for a discount. Our <a href="/best-rowing-machines/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">rowing machine guide</a> compares the main types if you are undecided between air, water and magnetic.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>5. Recovery and accessories last.</strong> A massage gun is pleasant and often
            heavily discounted, which is exactly why it tends to end up in baskets ahead of the
            equipment it is meant to support. Buy it once the rest is in place, or when it reaches
            a price that makes it an easy decision.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How We Chose These Seven</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these products. Every pick comes from one of this site's existing
            home gym roundups, written between 13 and 19 October 2026, where each product was
            confirmed as currently sold and its figures were checked against the maker and at least
            one independent source. We reused those verified figures here rather than collecting new
            ones, so that this page agrees with the roundups it draws on.
          </p>
          <p>
            Four rules decided the list. <strong>One product per category,</strong> so that the page
            is a map of a home gym rather than seven variations on one machine. <strong>Sold on
            Amazon US,</strong> preferring products with a verified Amazon listing; that ruled out
            equipment sold only direct, such as Rogue's racks and much of REP's strength range,
            which would only send you to a "similar" search. <strong>No open safety
            action,</strong> which ruled out the recalled products listed above. And <strong>a dated price we could stand behind,</strong> recorded on Amazon or the maker's
            store on a stated date, rather than a figure we would have to guess.
          </p>
          <p>
            The target prices are the part of this page that is opinion. Each is the point at which
            we would stop waiting, based on the price we recorded, how that category usually
            discounts and, where we have it, the price the product has already sold for this
            month. They are not forecasts, and a retailer may beat them or never reach them.
          </p>
          <p>
            A few close calls are worth naming. Core Home Fitness's twist-handle dumbbells, also on
            Amazon at around $380 a pair in our October check, are the cheaper alternative to the
            QuickDraw. Titan's T-3 short squat stand, $300.97 on sale on 16 October, suits low
            ceilings better than the PR-1100. The Hyperice Hypervolt 3, about $249 on 19 October, is
            a quieter alternative to the Theragun. And Sunny Health &amp; Fitness's dual-rail magnetic
            rower, about $320 to $400, is the budget alternative to the Concept2.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying Home Gym Equipment on Black Friday</h2>
          <p>Most of these come from buying in the order the sales suggest rather than the order a home gym needs.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Waiting on equipment that barely discounts</h3>
              <p>Holding off on dumbbells, plates or a Concept2 rower for a month in the hope of a big cut usually saves little and costs weeks of training. If a category rarely discounts by more than about 10%, buy it when you need it.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Judging the deal by the badge</h3>
              <p>A percentage off is measured from whatever reference price the seller chooses. The Schwinn IC4 sold at $799 and $899 within three days in October, so "$100 off $999" in November would be no deal. Compare against the price you recorded yourself.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Ignoring the delivered price</h3>
              <p>Freight surcharges, kerbside-only delivery and paid assembly can wipe out a discount on a treadmill, bike or rack. Compare delivered prices across Amazon and the brand store, and know how far the box will travel before you order.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying recalled or superseded models cheaply</h3>
              <p>Sale periods clear old stock and fill the second-hand market. Recalled BowFlex SelectTech dumbbells, NordicTrack RW900 rowers and older Peloton bikes all circulate. Check the exact model and serial number at cpsc.gov before you pay.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            If you are starting a home gym this November, buy the things that rarely discount first —
            a pair of <strong class="text-white">REP QuickDraw</strong> or Core dumbbells and a rubber
            mat — and watch the sales for the cardio machine. Our targets are <strong class="text-white">about $600 for the Horizon T101 Connect</strong> and <strong class="text-white">$799 for the Schwinn IC4</strong>, and the <strong class="text-white">Concept2 RowErg</strong> is worth buying at $990 whenever you
            are ready.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whatever you buy, record today's price, check the seller and the model, compare delivered
            prices and steer clear of recalled equipment. Our <a href="/treadmill-black-friday-deals/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">Black Friday deal method</a> explains how to judge any offer, and the <a href="/home-gym/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">home gym section</a> has a full roundup for every category on this page.
          </p>
        </div>

